<title> Management of Patient with Pneumothorax (PTX).</title>
<graphic href="eee0819-w0001.gif" />
<cell type="start" id="cell-1">
<label>Patient with pneumothorax (PTX). Give oxygen and assess severity. Small PTX and stable patient?</label>
<path target="cell-2">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-4">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-2">
<label>Primary PTX?</label>
<path target="cell-3">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-5">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-3">
<label>Observation.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-4">
<label>Large PTX or unstable patient. Primary or secondary PTX?</label>
<path target="cell-5">Primary</path>
<path target="cell-6">Secondary</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-5">
<label>Aspiration or small-bore chest tube.*</label>
<path target="cell-7"></path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-6">
<label>Chest tube.*</label>
<path target="cell-7"></path>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-7">
<label>Air leak present?</label>
<path target="cell-8">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-9">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-8">
<label>Continue to monitor for 4 days; if not resolved, proceed to surgical intervention.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-9">
<label>Remove chest tube, with or without clamp tube before removal.</label>
</cell>
<footnotes>
<footnote>*If chest tube, connect to water seal with or without suction.</footnote>
<footnote>Adapted from Baumann M. Management of spontaneous pneumothorax. <em>Clin Chest Med</em> 2006;<strong>27</strong>:369-381.<xref ref="pubmed16716824" resource="acc001" />
</footnote>
</footnotes>
</algorithm>
